%%%
For a complete real Hilbert space $`H`, the canonical isometric map

$$`
\iota_H:H\longrightarrow(H^*)^*,\qquad
\iota_H(x)(\ell)=\ell(x),
`

is surjective. Thus $`H` is reflexive in the functional-analytic sense used
by the direct method.
:::

:::proof "analysis.weak_compactness.hilbert_reflexive"
Pull a double-dual functional back along the Fréchet–Riesz isometric
equivalence between the Hilbert space and its continuous dual. Applying the
inverse equivalence produces a vector whose canonical double-dual image is the
original functional.
:::

Reflexivity feeds the compactness statements: it identifies the weak topology
of $`H` with the weak-star topology of its double dual, where Banach–Alaoglu
applies. The norm-bound and Radon–Riesz estimates use something more
elementary, namely the defining property of weak convergence.

:::theorem "analysis.weak_compactness.evaluation" (parent := "methods.direct_method") (lean := "PDE.Analysis.WeakCompactness.tendsto_evaluation")
%%%
