%%%
A weakly closed subset of a complete real Hilbert space is weakly compact when
its underlying normed-space set is bounded:

$$`
K\subset H,\qquad K\ \text{weakly closed and norm bounded}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad K\ \text{weakly compact}.
`

The Lean theorem is stated for Mathlib's weak-space topology and proves
topological compactness.
:::

:::proof "analysis.weak_compactness.bounded_closed" (uses := "analysis.weak_compactness.hilbert_reflexive")
Hilbert reflexivity identifies the weak space with the range of its canonical
weak-star double-dual embedding. Banach–Alaoglu gives compactness of the bounded
weak-star closure, and the embedding transfers compactness back to the weak
topology. Weak closedness removes the closure operation.
:::

Compactness of a sublevel set does not by itself keep a weak limit inside a
constrained admissible class. For linear constraints, weak continuity of the
constraint maps does.

:::theorem "analysis.weak_compactness.linear_constraints" (parent := "methods.direct_method") (lean := "PDE.Analysis.tendsto_weak_map, PDE.Analysis.isClosed_weakConstraintSet, PDE.Analysis.constraints_eq_zero_of_tendsto_weak, PDE.Analysis.WeakCompactness.isCompact_inter_weakConstraintSet") (uses := "analysis.weak_compactness.bounded_closed")
