%%%
Let $`A_j:H\to K` be any family of continuous linear maps. Their simultaneous
kernel

$$`
\mathcal C=\bigcap_j\ker A_j
`

is weakly closed. Consequently, if $`x_\alpha\rightharpoonup x` and,
eventually, $`A_jx_\alpha=0` for all $`j` simultaneously, then
$`A_jx=0` for every $`j`. If $`S\subset H` is weakly closed and norm bounded,
then $`S\cap\mathcal C` is weakly compact.

The same argument preserves, under weak limits, incompressibility, zero mean,
homogeneous traces, and other linear PDE side conditions once the relevant
operators are continuous.
:::

:::proof "analysis.weak_compactness.linear_constraints" (uses := "analysis.weak_compactness.bounded_closed")
Every continuous linear map $`A_j` is continuous from the weak topology of
$`H` to the weak topology of $`K`: composing a continuous functional on $`K`
with $`A_j` gives a continuous functional on $`H`. Hence each kernel is weakly
closed, and arbitrary intersections preserve closedness. A weak limit that is
eventually in this intersection remains in it. Finally, intersecting with a
weakly closed bounded set preserves closedness and boundedness, so Hilbert
weak compactness applies.
:::

A weak limit produced by compactness comes with no size information of its
own. The evaluation property supplies it, and in the direct method this keeps
the minimizer inside the same ball as the minimizing sequence.

:::theorem "analysis.weak_compactness.norm_bound" (parent := "methods.direct_method") (lean := "PDE.Analysis.WeakCompactness.norm_le_of_tendsto_of_norm_le") (uses := "analysis.weak_compactness.evaluation")
