%%%
In any real normed space, if $`x_\alpha\rightharpoonup x` and eventually
$`\lVert x_\alpha\rVert\leq C`, then

$$`
\lVert x\rVert\leq C.
`

This is the bounded-net form of weak lower semicontinuity of the norm;
neither completeness nor reflexivity is needed.
:::

:::proof "analysis.weak_compactness.norm_bound" (uses := "analysis.weak_compactness.evaluation")
Hahn–Banach supplies a continuous linear functional $`\ell` with
$`\lVert\ell\rVert\leq1` and $`\ell(x)=\lVert x\rVert`. The eventual norm
bound gives $`\ell(x_\alpha)\leq C`. Weak convergence passes this inequality
to the limit, yielding $`\lVert x\rVert\leq C`. The norming-functional
statement also covers $`x=0`.
:::

Norm can be lost in a weak limit, as an orthonormal sequence tending weakly to
zero shows. The Radon–Riesz property says that this loss is the only
obstruction to strong convergence.

:::theorem "analysis.weak_compactness.radon_riesz" (parent := "methods.direct_method") (lean := "PDE.Analysis.WeakCompactness.tendsto_strong_of_norm") (uses := "analysis.weak_compactness.evaluation")
%%%
