%%%
Weak convergence in a real Hilbert space, together with convergence of the
norms to the norm of the weak limit, implies strong convergence:

$$`
x_\alpha\rightharpoonup x,\qquad
\lVert x_\alpha\rVert\longrightarrow\lVert x\rVert
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\lVert x_\alpha-x\rVert\longrightarrow0.
`

We use this Radon–Riesz property of Hilbert spaces when an energy identity
upgrades a weakly obtained limit or trace to norm convergence.
:::

:::proof "analysis.weak_compactness.radon_riesz" (uses := "analysis.weak_compactness.evaluation")
Expand the squared distance:

$$`
\|x_\alpha-x\|^2
=\|x_\alpha\|^2+\|x\|^2-2\langle x_\alpha,x\rangle.
`

Weak convergence gives
$`\langle x_\alpha,x\rangle\to\langle x,x\rangle`, while the additional
norm hypothesis gives $`\|x_\alpha\|^2\to\|x\|^2`. All three terms are now
controlled, and their limit is zero. For an orthonormal sequence the mixed
term still converges, but the squared norms stay at one; that is precisely
the missing hypothesis.
:::
